{#
    A picture, and the claim under it.

    The core writes a bare `<figure>` with the image inside it and a
    `<figcaption>` under it — correct markup, and nothing in this system: no
    frame around the picture, no ground under one that does not fill it, and a
    caption set as running text at the size of the prose beside it. That is the
    one thing this theme exists to stop, so the node comes out as `sds-figure`,
    which is where those three are decided for everything else that shows a
    picture.

    The picture is drawn into the page by the finishing step — `soul-finish.js`,
    over the rendered output — and not by a template writing the element's own
    markup. That is what lets this address the component: a figure whose picture
    waits for a script would be a caption under an empty box for a reader who
    runs none, and by the time the page is published there is no waiting left.

    `sds-art` is what makes it sit in the frame — one rule shared by the drawing
    and the photograph, because how a picture fills its surface is the same
    question for both. `align` is not carried over: a figure in a measure this
    narrow has nothing to float beside, which is the same answer the sidebar
    gets.

    A drawing of one's own is referenced and everything else is linked, which
    is the distinction `sds-image` makes and it is made here for the same
    reason: an SVG referenced into the page is drawn in the page's tokens and
    follows it into dark, while an `<img>` renders in a document of its own
    where none of them are declared. It costs the file one line — `id="art"` on
    its root — and `docs/design-system/artwork.rst` says what else it has to do.
    A drawing that never paid it is shown as an image instead: the finishing
    step reads the file, and a picture in its exported colours beats a hole in
    the page. It does not follow the reader into dark, and the frame under it
    does not either — see `--surface-art`.

    A file somewhere else is linked whatever it is: a reference reads the file
    itself, which a browser will not do across origins.
#}
{%- set caption = node.document ? renderNode(node.document)|striptags|trim : '' -%}
{%- set source = node.image.value is external_target ? node.image.value : asset(node.image.value) -%}
{#- A picture is drawn at the width of the column it stands in, which is not
    the width a diagram was drawn for. `:zoomable:` gives that back — the frame
    becomes the press and the viewer carries the claim into its own head — and
    the author writes it, because most pictures are read where they stand. The
    option is read off the figure: the core hands the image node a fixed list
    and one it has never heard of is not on it. -#}
<sds-figure
    src="{{ source }}"
    alt="{{ node.image.option('alt')|default('') }}"
    {%- if caption %} caption="{{ caption }}"{% endif -%}
    {%- if node.image.hasOption('width') %} width="{{ node.image.option('width') }}"{% endif -%}
    {%- if node.image.hasOption('height') %} height="{{ node.image.option('height') }}"{% endif -%}
    {%- if node.hasOption('zoomable') %} zoomable{% endif -%}
    {%- if node.image.hasOption('figclass') %} class="{{ node.image.option('figclass') }}"{% endif -%}
></sds-figure>
